    "summary": "Overloading & Pass-by-Value",
    "content": "### Java Methods & Overloading\n\nA method is a block of code executing an operation when invoked.\n\n#### Method Overloading\nMultiple methods in the same class can share the exact same name provided their **parameter lists differ** (in number of parameters, parameter types, or parameter ordering). Return type alone does not differentiate method signatures.\n\n#### Pass-by-Value Semantics\nJava is strictly **pass-by-value**:\n- For primitive types, the actual value is copied into the method parameter.\n- For objects, the reference pointer address is passed by value (the underlying object state can be mutated, but reassigning the parameter reference does not affect the caller).\n",
    "exampleCode": "public class Main {\n    // Method Overloading\n    public static int add(int a, int b) {\n        return a + b;\n    }\n\n    public static double add(double a, double b) {\n        return a + b;\n    }\n\n    public static int add(int a, int b, int c) {\n        return a + b + c;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(\"add(5, 10): \" + add(5, 10));\n        System.out.println(\"add(3.5, 4.2): \" + add(3.5, 4.2));\n        System.out.println(\"add(1, 2, 3): \" + add(1, 2, 3));\n    }\n}",
